{% sec 1, "First Sale vs Resale", "What's the difference between selling something new and selling it on?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think about concert tickets. When a band announces a tour, it sells the tickets itself. You pay \(\text{£}50\) on the official site and the \(\text{£}50\) goes to the band. That's the <strong>first sale</strong>.</p>
    <p>A week later you find you can't go, so you sell your ticket to another fan on a resale site for \(\text{£}80\). That \(\text{£}80\) goes to <em>you</em>. The band gets nothing, even though it's their concert. That's the <strong>resale</strong>.</p>
    <p>Shares and bonds work exactly the same way. A company or government creates them and sells them for the first time to raise money. That happens in the {% gl "primary market" %}primary market{% endgl %}, and whoever created them, the {% gl "issuer" %}issuer{% endgl %}, gets the cash. From then on, investors sell them to each other, over and over, in the {% gl "secondary market" %}secondary market{% endgl %}. The issuer gets nothing from those trades.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Where the money goes</h3><p>Top: the first sale. Bottom: every trade after it.</p></div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-flow" viewBox="0 0 640 400" role="img" aria-label="In the primary market the company sells new shares through an investment bank and gets the money. In the secondary market investors trade with each other through an exchange and the company gets nothing."></svg>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Almost everything you see on a trading screen is the secondary market. When the news says "shares in a company fell \(5\%\) today", that's investors trading with each other. The company's bank balance didn't move at all.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Primary market: the first sale, and the issuer gets the money. Secondary market: every sale after that, and the money just moves between investors.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "How a First Sale Works", "How do new shares and bonds actually get sold?", "How a first sale works" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Shares: the IPO</h3>
    <p>When a company sells shares to the public for the first time, it's called an {% gl "ipo" %}IPO{% endgl %}, an initial public offering. It's also called "going public" or "listing", because afterwards its shares are listed on a stock exchange where anyone can buy them.</p>
    <p>The company doesn't do this alone. It hires investment banks, called {% gl "underwriter" %}underwriters{% endgl %}, to run the sale. Roughly, it goes like this:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Get ready.</strong> The banks help the company write a long document explaining the business, its numbers and its risks, so buyers know what they're getting.</li>
      <li><strong>Find the buyers.</strong> The banks take the company on a tour of big investors (pension funds, fund managers, hedge funds) and ask each one how many shares they'd want, and at what price. Collecting these orders is called {% gl "bookbuilding" %}bookbuilding{% endgl %}.</li>
      <li><strong>Set the price.</strong> Using the orders in the book, the banks and the company agree a price, usually a little below what the book suggests, so the shares are easy to sell and buyers are happy.</li>
      <li><strong>First day of trading.</strong> The shares start trading on the exchange. From this moment, it's the secondary market.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If the price jumps on the first day, that's called a "pop". It feels like good news, but think about who it's good for. The investors who bought at the IPO price made a quick profit. The company sold its shares for less than people were willing to pay. The gap is called <strong>money left on the table</strong>.</p>
    <p>Companies can also sell more new shares years later. That's called a follow-on offering, and it's still the primary market, because new shares are being created and the company gets the cash.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: the auction</h3>
    <p>Governments raise money by selling bonds, and most do it by {% gl "auction" %}auction{% endgl %}. In the UK, a government agency called the Debt Management Office announces how much it wants to borrow and for how long. Banks and big investors put in bids, and the bonds go to the best bids. UK government bonds are called {% gl "gilt" %}gilts{% endgl %}. Companies selling bonds usually go through banks instead, a bit like a smaller IPO.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Follow the money</h3><p>A company with \(4\) million shares, all owned by its founders, sells \(1\) million brand-new shares in an IPO. Set the IPO price, then move the share price in the resale market afterwards.</p></div>
