{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Why the Resale Market Matters", "If the company gets nothing from it, why does it care?", "Why resale matters" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>If the company only gets money at the first sale, you might think it wouldn't care what happens afterwards. It cares a lot, for three reasons.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Nobody would buy at the first sale without a resale market.<span class="why">If you could never sell your shares, you'd be stuck with them forever. Knowing you can get out whenever you like ({% gl "liquidity" %}liquidity{% endgl %}) is what makes people willing to buy in the first place, and to pay a better price for it.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>The next first sale depends on it.<span class="why">When a company wants to raise more money later, it sells new shares at around the current price. If the price has risen, it raises more for every share it gives away. If it has fallen, raising the same money means giving away more of the company.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The owners and bosses are usually paid in shares.<span class="why">Founders keep most of their shares after the IPO, and managers are often paid partly in shares. The resale price decides how wealthy they are.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The same is true for governments. If investors are selling its bonds in the secondary market, their prices fall and the interest rate they pay rises. The government has to offer that higher rate at its next auction, so borrowing gets more expensive for it too.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The issuer only gets money at the first sale, but the resale market sets the price of every sale that comes after.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Primary market</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Where brand-new shares or bonds are sold for the first time. The issuer gets the money.</p><p class="eg">An IPO or a government bond auction.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Secondary market</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Where investors trade existing shares and bonds with each other. The issuer gets nothing.</p><p class="eg">Almost all trading on the London Stock Exchange.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Issuer</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Whoever creates and first sells a share or bond: a company or a government.</p><p class="eg">The UK government is the issuer of gilts.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>IPO</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Initial public offering: a company's first sale of shares to the public, when it lists on an exchange.</p><p class="eg">"The IPO was ten times oversubscribed."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Underwriter</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The investment bank that runs a new issue, finds buyers and helps set the price.</p><p class="eg">Banks usually take a fee of a few percent of what's raised.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Bookbuilding</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Collecting orders from big investors (how many, at what price) to decide the price of a new issue.</p><p class="eg">"The book's covered" means there are enough orders to sell everything.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Gilt</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A bond issued by the UK government. Sold at auctions run by the Debt Management Office.</p><p class="eg">"The 10-year gilt auction went well."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Market capitalisation</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>What the whole company is worth at today's share price: the price times the number of shares.</p><p class="eg">\(5\) million shares at \(\text{£}10\) is a \(\text{£}50\) million market cap.</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where first sales and resales turn up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: my three markets</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bonds</strong> have a huge primary market, because governments borrow all the time. Auction days matter to traders: if an auction gets weak demand, the government has to accept a higher interest rate, and bond prices across the whole secondary market can drop with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Gold</strong> has a tiny first sale: mines selling newly dug gold. Almost all the gold ever mined still exists, so nearly all gold trading is resale of bars that already exist.</li>
        <li><strong>FX</strong> doesn't really have a first sale at all. Currencies are created by central banks and banks, not sold to investors in an IPO. Every FX trade is two people swapping money that already exists, so it's all secondary market.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most trading jobs, including FX and commodities sales and trading, sit in the secondary market. The primary market is mostly the job of capital markets teams inside investment banks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: off-plan vs resale</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Buying a brand-new home from a developer, often before it's even built ("off-plan"), is the primary market: the developer gets the money. Buying a home from its last owner is the secondary market. Developers raise money for the next building by selling the current one, so a strong resale market for their homes makes their next project easier to fund, just like a company's share price.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: getting in at the first sale</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Nigeria's Debt Management Office sells government bonds at regular auctions, just like the UK's. It also sells savings bonds aimed at ordinary people, in much smaller amounts. Companies list on the Nigerian Exchange in Lagos.</p>
      <p>Historically, first sales have mostly gone to banks and big investors, while ordinary savers only got in later, in the secondary market. A big opportunity for fintech is letting people take part in first sales straight from their phones, in small amounts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "The Maths Underneath", "How much does a first sale raise, and who gains when the price moves?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>How much is raised</h3>
    <p>If \(N\) new shares are sold at price \(P\), and the banks take a fee of \(f\) (as a fraction), the company receives:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Money raised</span>\[ \text{Gross} = P \times N \qquad \text{Net} = P \times N \times (1 - f) \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>What the whole company is worth</h3>
    <p>After the IPO, there are the old shares plus the new ones. The market capitalisation is the share price times all of them. New shares also make the founders' slice smaller, which is called dilution.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Market cap and ownership</span>\[ \text{Market cap} = P \times (\text{old shares} + \text{new shares}) \qquad \text{Founders' share} = \frac{\text{old shares}}{\text{old shares} + \text{new shares}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Money left on the table</h3>
    <p>If the shares close their first day at \(P_1\) after being sold at the IPO price \(P_0\):</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The first-day pop</span>\[ \text{Money left on the table} = (P_1 - P_0) \times N \qquad \text{First-day pop} = \frac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0} \]</div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 1</span>
    <h3>A company sells \(1\) million new shares at \(\text{£}10\). The banks charge \(3\%\). How much does it get?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Gross: \(10 \times 1{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}10{,}000{,}000\).</li>
      <li>Net: \(10{,}000{,}000 \times (1 - 0.03) = \text{£}9{,}700{,}000\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The company gets \(\text{£}9.7\) million. The \(\text{£}300{,}000\) fee goes to the banks. (The \(3\%\) is just an example.)</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 2</span>
    <h3>The founders owned all \(4\) million old shares. After the IPO, what's the company worth at \(\text{£}10\), and how much do the founders own?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Total shares: \(4\text{m} + 1\text{m} = 5\text{m}\).</li>
      <li>Market cap: \(10 \times 5{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}50{,}000{,}000\).</li>
      <li>Founders' share: \(\frac{4}{5} = 80\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The founders gave up \(20\%\) of the company in exchange for \(\text{£}10\) million of cash for the business.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 3</span>
    <h3>The shares close their first day at \(\text{£}13\). How much was left on the table?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\((13 - 10) \times 1{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}3{,}000{,}000\).</li>
      <li>First-day pop: \(\frac{13 - 10}{10} = 30\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Buyers were willing to pay \(\text{£}13\) million for shares the company sold for \(\text{£}10\) million. That \(\text{£}3\) million went to the IPO investors instead of the company. It's exactly what the "follow the money" chart shows at its starting settings.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>A company sells \(5\) million new shares at \(\text{£}8\) and pays its banks a \(4\%\) fee. How much does it receive?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Gross: \(8 \times 5{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}40{,}000{,}000\).</span><span>Net: \(40{,}000{,}000 \times 0.96 = \text{£}38{,}400{,}000\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Founders own \(9\) million shares. The company sells \(3\) million new shares at \(\text{£}12\). What's the market cap, and what percentage do the founders now own?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Market cap: \(12 \times 12{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}144{,}000{,}000\).</span><span>Founders: \(\frac{9}{12} = 75\%\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>An IPO sells \(4\) million shares at \(\text{£}15\). They close day one at \(\text{£}18\). How much was left on the table, and what was the first-day pop?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\((18 - 15) \times 4{,}000{,}000 = \text{£}12{,}000{,}000\).</span><span>Pop: \(\frac{3}{15} = 20\%\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>You buy a new-build flat from a developer. Ten years later you sell it to a family. Which sale was primary and which was secondary?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Buying from the developer was the primary market: the flat was being sold for the first time and the developer got the money. Selling to the family was the secondary market: the developer got nothing.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
